 522, 860, 919, 1085, 1390, 1431, 1589, 1672, 1860, 1936], "window": [2551, 32551], "confidence": 0.87, "evidence": [{"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_03", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_09", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_10", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "At the prescribed half-window of 5000, peak before/after dissimilarity is only 0.2105 (t~25499), well under the 0.6 threshold, and the series rises smoothly from 0.03 to 0.21 \u2014 gradual drift. A 0.6129 peak at bandwidth 2500 is an artifact: its after-set is a strict subset of its before-set and events drop 83->12 across that boundary, so the fall in Jaccard reflects an activity trough rather than arrival of new partners. An edge-level Jaccard of 0.0 between the first and last windows is also misleading: it is computed over node pairs, and nodes 214 (early) and 1860 (late) contact the identical 30-node pool, showing the group's external contacts are stable while only the active emitter rotates. No bursts detected. Group volume and degree are ample, so the negative is not from sparsity."}, "raw_final_response": "## Analysis\n\n**The prescribed measurement (half-window 5000):** peak dissimilarity = **0.2105** at t\u224825499, far below the 0.6 threshold (step_01, step_13). Turnover rises smoothly from 0.03 \u2192 0.21 across 30k time units \u2014 the signature of gradual drift, not a switch.\n\n**Three alternative explanations tested and ruled out:**\n\n1. **Insufficient data?** No \u2014 each of the 12 nodes has ~90 events and ~31 distinct partners (step_03), and unique-edge counts stay in the 30\u201370 range in every bin (step_04). The group is amply observed.\n\n2. **A real switch hidden at finer resolution?** At bandwidth 2500 a peak of 0.6129 appears at t\u224816589 (step_06) \u2014 but this is an artifact, not a regime switch. The \"after\" contact set `[123\u2026945]` is a *strict subset* of the \"before\" set, i.e. contacts vanished without any new ones arriving. Step_07 shows why: events collapse from 83 to 12 across that boundary. It is an activity trough that starves the after-window (e.g. node 1672 has just a single neighbour there, step_08), so Jaccard falls purely from missing samples. A genuine regime switch requires *new* partners, not merely fewer.\n\n3. **The edge-level `jaccard: 0.0` between the first and last windows (step_09)?** This looks damning but is measured over node *pairs*, not contact sets. Steps 11 and 12 resolve it: node 214 early contacts exactly `[123, 160, 208, \u2026, 1950]`, and node 1860 late contacts **that identical 30-node set**. The external contact pool is unchanged; what rotates is which group member is currently the active emitter. Edges churn, partners do not \u2014 so the group's contact neighbourhood never actually turns over. Step_10 confirms no bursts (0 bursts, z\u22651.5), consistent with a stationary regime.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_8b4a3bfa\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [214, 315, 522, 860, 919, 1085, 1390, 1431, 1589, 1672, 1860, 1936],\n  \"window\": [2551, 32551],\n  \"confidence\": 0.87,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"At the prescribed half-window of 5000, peak before/after dissimilarity is only 0.2105 (t~25499), well under the 0.6 threshold, and the series rises smoothly from 0.03 to 0.21 \u2014 gradual drift. A 0.6129 peak at bandwidth 2500 is an artifact: its after-set is a strict subset of its before-set and events drop 83->12 across that boundary, so the fall in Jaccard reflects an activity trough rather than arrival of new partners. An edge-level Jaccard of 0.0 between the first and last windows is also misleading: it is computed over node pairs, and nodes 214 (early) and 1860 (late) contact the identical 30-node pool, showing the group's external contacts are stable while only the active emitter rotates. No bursts detected. Group volume and degree are ample, so the negative is not from sparsity.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 67348, "total_output_tokens": 4815, "total_tokens": 72163, "total_latency_ms": 72575, "llm_calls": 8, "schema_repairs": 0, "tool_protocol_errors": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}